# Production settings for the office install (§2.3, §12).
#
#   cd deploy && cp .env.example .env
#   # fill in the two secrets below, then:
#   docker compose up -d
#
# It must be called exactly `.env`: Compose auto-loads only that name for `${...}` substitution in
# the compose file. Any other name and `DATA_ROOT` silently resolves to nothing, mounting the
# office's documents inside the container instead of on the Desktop.
#
# `.env` is gitignored and MUST stay that way — it holds the database password and the
# signing key. Keep a copy on the external drive beside the images: a restored database cannot be
# opened without it, and a new SECRET_KEY invalidates every signed token (§13.3).

# ---- secrets: generate these, never reuse the examples ----
# The name is `DJANGO_SECRET_KEY`, not `SECRET_KEY` — the app refuses to boot without it when
# DEBUG is off, which is how the wrong name here was caught (2026-08-11).
# python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(50))"
DJANGO_SECRET_KEY=CHANGE-ME-generate-a-50-character-random-string
# python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(24))"
# ** LETTERS AND DIGITS ONLY if you make one up by hand — no `#`, no `@`. `#` starts a comment in
# some .env parsers, so the value truncates silently, and Postgres bakes the password into the data
# volume on FIRST start: finding out later means wiping the database. (Hit live, 2026-08-13.)
# The generator above is safe — token_urlsafe emits neither character.
DB_PASSWORD=CHANGE-ME-generate-a-random-password

# ---- database ----
DB_NAME=landalloc
DB_USER=landalloc
DB_HOST=db
DB_PORT=5432

# ---- Django ----
DJANGO_DEBUG=0
# Every hostname or IP the app is reached by. **Both office computers must be listed**, or the one
# that is not gets a 400 with no explanation. Django does not enforce this when DEBUG is on, which
# is exactly why it is caught in production and not in dev.
DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS=localhost,127.0.0.1,192.168.1.10
# Same origin serves the SPA and the API through Nginx, so no cross-origin allowance is needed.
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# ---- Celery ----
CELERY_BROKER_URL=redis://redis:6379/0

# ---- data ----
# Where the office's documents and database dumps live — a real folder they can open and copy to
# the external drive (§2.5). Set it explicitly: `~` is not expanded reliably, and Windows has none.
#   Windows: DATA_ROOT=C:/Users/<user>/Desktop/LandAllocationData
#   macOS:   DATA_ROOT=/Users/<user>/Desktop/LandAllocationData
DATA_ROOT=/Users/CHANGE-ME/Desktop/LandAllocationData

# Inside the container these are fixed; they are the mount targets in docker-compose.yml.
DOCUMENTS_ROOT=/data/documents
DB_BACKUP_ROOT=/data/db-backups

# ---- read by docker-compose.yml, not by the app ----
# These two are `${...}` substitutions in the compose file. That is exactly why this file must be
# named `.env`: any other name and they resolve to empty, mounting the archive inside the container.
# Port the office browses to. 80 needs no port in the URL; change it if something else uses it.
WEB_PORT=80
# ---- tuning: every one of these has a sane default; set them only to change it ----
# Failed sign-ins per IP before the desk is locked out for a minute (§12).
LOGIN_THROTTLE_RATE=10/min
# Session lengths (§7.1). A refresh token left on a shared office machine lasts this long, which
# is the trade recorded there — shorten it if the office would rather sign in more often.
ACCESS_TOKEN_MINUTES=30
REFRESH_TOKEN_DAYS=7
# Largest single upload the app accepts, in bytes. Nginx allows 64 MB; this is the real limit.
MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=26214400
